=== task: multi easy
title: True of selection sort
prompt: Choose **every** true statement about selection sort.
* After pass k, the first k items are the k smallest, in order :: Right. Each pass puts the next smallest item in place.
* It makes at most n − 1 swaps :: Right. It swaps once per pass, and there are n − 1 passes.
* It makes the same number of comparisons on every list of n items :: Right. Every pass scans the whole unsorted part: n × (n − 1) / 2 comparisons.
- It stops early when the list is already sorted :: It has no early stop. It can't know an item is the smallest without comparing it with all the others.
- It only swaps neighbours :: Its swap can move an item across the whole list.
- It's stable :: A long swap can move an item past an equal one.
hint: How many swaps does one pass make?
hint: Does a sorted list let any pass skip its scan?
right: The sorted part grows by one item per pass, with one swap per pass, and every list of n items costs n × (n − 1) / 2 comparisons. Selection sort has no early stop and isn't stable.
wrong: Selection sort makes one swap per pass, which can move an item far, past equal items, so it isn't stable. It always scans the whole unsorted part, so it never stops early.

