wrong[['apple', 'fig', 'Fig', 'pear']]: Capital letters come before lowercase ones, so "Fig" is the smallest of all.
wrong[['Fig', 'fig', 'apple', 'pear']]: "Fig" and "fig" aren't equal, and "apple" comes between them, because "a" is smaller than "f".
hint: `<` compares strings by their first different character.
hint: In Python's order, "A" to "Z" all come before "a" to "z".
right: `['Fig', 'apple', 'fig', 'pear']`: "Fig" comes first because of its capital F; the rest are in alphabetical order.
wrong: Capital letters sort before lowercase letters, so "Fig" is the smallest. Then "apple", "fig" and "pear" are compared by their first letters, a, f and p.

